  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an integer NNN. Let KKK be a divisor of NNN **of your choice** such that K&gt;1K &gt; 1K&gt;1, and let M=NKM = \\frac{N}{K}M=KN​. You need to find the **smallest** KKK such that MMM has as many divisors as possible.\n\n**Note**: UUU is a divisor of VVV if VVV is divisible by UUU.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of the input contains an integer TTT - the number of test cases. The test cases then follow.\nThe only line of each test case contains an integer NNN.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output in a single line minimum value of KKK such that MMM has as many divisors as possible.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤30001 \\leq T \\leq 30001≤T≤3000",
      "2≤N≤1092 \\leq N \\leq 10^92≤N≤109"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n4\n6",
        "output": "3\n2\n2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: The only possible value for KKK is 333, and that is the answer.\nTest case 222: There are two cases:\n\nK=2K = 2K=2. Then M=42=2M = \\frac{4}{2} = 2M=24​=2, which has 222 divisors (111 and 222).\nK=4K = 4K=4. Then M=44=1M = \\frac{4}{4} = 1M=44​=1, which has 111 divisor (111).\n\n\n\nTherefore the answer is 222.\n\nTest case 333: There are three cases:\n\nK=2K = 2K=2. Then M=62=3M = \\frac{6}{2} = 3M=26​=3, which has 222 divisors (111 and 333).\nK=3K = 3K=3. Then M=63=2M = \\frac{6}{3} = 2M=36​=2, which has 222 divisors (111 and 222).\nK=6K = 6K=6. Then M=66=1M = \\frac{6}{6} = 1M=66​=1, which has 111 divisor (111).\n\n\n\nTherefore the answer is 222."
      }
    ]
  },
